From: u185f4d4fdb048fb8 at groups.google.com (Henrik Bengtsson)
Date: Tue, 21 Dec 2010 18:16:10 +0000
Subject: Re: [aroma.affymetrix] fracB and BAF values
Message-ID: <WP5KRi69U0k.CZZJu3IXGfIJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <WP5KRi69U0k.WNVvue94KyAJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
References: <WP5KRi69U0k.WNVvue94KyAJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/aroma-affymetrix/c/WP5KRi69U0k/m/CZZJu3IXGfIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi.

On Mon, Dec 20, 2010 at 8:51 AM, allab <asph...@googlemail.com> wrote:
> Dear aroma users/authors,
> i am doing now Affy 6.0 SNP data analysis and my goal is to become BAF
> values (B-allele frequency) as they were output from Illumina
> software.
> BAF is a normalized metric which reflects the proportion of B-alleles
> in each SNP, e.g. 0 for AA SNP, 0.5 for an AB SNP and 1 for BB.

Correct.

> I have generated fracB values with aroma package and see now that
> there are also negative values for fracB, but as i understand it
> correctly BAF values should be in the range between 0 and 1.

The thing is that the *true* BAF signals should be in [0,1].  However,
with noise, it is possible that some of the estimates can have values
outside this.  Here BAF is defined as:

BAF = C_B / (C_A + C_B)

It is unlikely (but not impossible) that the *estimated* total CN C =
C_A + C_B is negative.  However, most likely, the reason why you are
observing negative BAFs is that C_B is estimated to be negative.  This
typically happen when we do allelic crosstalk correction (see the CRMA
paper Bengtsson et al. 2008;
http://www.aroma-project.org/publications/).  More specifically,
consider a AA gene where the true C_B signal is zero.   Due to
crosstalk some of the signal of C_A is picked up by the B probe and
C_B is somewhat greater than zero.  Crosstalk calibration tries to
bring this back toward zero.  Due to noise and this correct, this way
some BB SNPs will end up one zero, some on the positive side and some
on the negative side.

For the same reason, you'll find that AA SNPs may have BAFs greater than one.

> The question is, how can i convert fracB values into BAF values.

So nothing to convert.  If you want strictly [0,1] signals, the
truncate at 0 and 1.  However, ideally you're downstream model/method
handles the data without such "harsh" pruning (the Illumina methods
forces the BAFs to be within [0,1]).

/Henrik

